NpuCamFX for Debian/Ubuntu
==========================

This package installs NpuCamFX system-wide:

  /usr/bin/npueffectsd
  /usr/bin/npucam-preview
  /usr/bin/npu-effects-indicator
  /usr/lib/<multiarch>/gstreamer-1.0/libgstnpueffects.so
  /usr/lib/npu-camfx/openvino/        bundled OpenVINO runtime
  /usr/lib/systemd/user/              user service units
  /etc/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/
  /usr/share/npu-camfx/models/        segmentation and face models
  /etc/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/60-npu-camfx-hide-libcamera.conf

The package intentionally writes no application files into user home
directories. The camera stream and previews are runtime data under
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.

Requirements
------------

- /dev/video60 from v4l2loopback. NpuCamFX does not replace an existing
  module configuration; install v4l2loopback and make sure the device is
  available. See /usr/share/doc/npu-camfx/examples/60-npu-camfx.conf.
- An Intel NPU driver providing /dev/accel/accel0. The OpenVINO NPU
  plugin is bundled; the kernel driver and firmware are not.
- A working GStreamer libcamera source (`gstreamer1.0-libcamera`).
- The `ubuntu-appindicators@ubuntu.com` GNOME extension for the top-bar
  menu.

Enabling for the current session
--------------------------------

The units are enabled for future graphical sessions through
/etc/systemd/user. After installation, load them into the current user
manager and start them:

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user start npu-effects.service npu-effects-ui.service

They will start automatically at the next graphical login.

If you previously used the source install, run the migration helper once
as your desktop user:

  /usr/share/npu-camfx/migrate-user-install.sh

This removes the old copies from ~/.local and ~/.config/systemd/user and
starts the packaged units. It does not remove the OpenVINO virtual
environment or the development model cache.

Configuration
-------------

The daemon reads models from /usr/share/npu-camfx/models. The bundled
OpenVINO libraries are private to NpuCamFX and are loaded through the
unit's LD_LIBRARY_PATH and the binaries' RUNPATH.

The WirePlumber rule hides the raw libcamera source so portal-based
applications use the virtual camera. It matches the GC2607 node used
during development; edit the conffile if your camera node has a different
name.

Removal
-------

  sudo apt remove npu-camfx

User services stop at logout. The WirePlumber conffile is kept on remove
and deleted on purge. v4l2loopback, the NPU driver, and any development
virtual environment are not managed by this package.
